4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,4,4,2,2,2,4,4,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,2,2,2,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,4,4,2,2,2,4,4,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,4,4,3,3,3,4,4,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,4,4,9,9,9,4,4,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,9,9,9,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,4,4,9,9,9,4,4,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4,1,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is a regular lattice of dots on a background colour. A dot sits at every cell where both y and x are odd. A few lattice dots are recoloured to trace the outline of a rectangle; I'll call this the frame. Outside the lattice, at positions where both y and x are even, there are small loose pieces, each in its own colour.\n\n**What the outputs show**\n- **The frame becomes solid.** Its outline (pair 1 colour 4, pair 2 colour 1, pair 3 colour 3) is drawn as continuous lines along its four sides.\n- **The inside is cleared.** All lattice dots inside the frame turn to background.\n- **Each piece becomes a solid block.** The block is the piece's bounding box filled in. In pair 1, piece 2 has points at rows 16/18/20 and columns 6/8, which gives a 5×3 block. In pair 2, the single row of 9s at columns 20/22 gives a 1×3 bar.\n- **The pieces vanish from their old positions,** and the blocks are packed inside the frame:\n  - **Direction:** blocks go in a row if the frame is wider than it is tall (pairs 1 and 3), and in a column if it is taller (pair 2).\n  - **Order:** blocks keep the input order of the pieces along that axis (by x for a row, by y for a column).\n  - **Spacing:** there is one background cell between neighbouring blocks.\n  - **Position:** the group is centred inside the frame on both axes. Every pair has a 1-cell margin, which centring reproduces.\n\n**Checking the counts**\n- Pair 1: the interior width is 9 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 3 + 1.\n- Pair 2: the interior height is 11 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 + 1.\n- Pair 3: the interior width is 15 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 3 + 1.\n- Across the packing axis, each block is centred. For example, in pair 2 the 3-wide blocks sit at columns 8–10 inside interior columns 6–12.\n\n**Test input**\n- The frame is colour 8, at rows 3–15 and columns 3–11. It is taller than wide, so the blocks are stacked vertically.\n- The pieces, in y order, are:\n  - 2: a 3×3 block,\n  - 3: a 1×3 bar,\n  - 9: a 3×3 block.\n- These take 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 = 9 rows, centred in the 11 interior rows. That puts them at rows 5–7, 9 and 11–13, in columns 6–8.\n\n**How the program finds things**\n- The dot colour is the most common non-background colour.\n- The frame colour is the other colour that occupies odd–odd cells.\n- Pieces are the remaining colours.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    cols = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg]\n    dot = max(cols, key=lambda c: (g == c).sum())\n    odd = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    odd[1::2, 1::2] = True\n    frame_c = None\n    for c in cols:\n        if c != dot and ((g == c) & odd).sum() > 0:\n            frame_c = c\n    piece_cols = [c for c in cols if c not in (dot, frame_c)]\n    pieces = []\n    for c in piece_cols:\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        pieces.append((c, ys.min(), xs.min(), ys.max(), xs.max()))\n    ys, xs = np.where(g == frame_c)\n    fy0, fx0, fy1, fx1 = ys.min(), xs.min(), ys.max(), xs.max()\n    out = g.copy()\n    # remove pieces\n    for c in piece_cols:\n        out[out == c] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Remove the loose pieces from their original positions.\")\n    # solid frame\n    out[fy0, fx0:fx1 + 1] = frame_c\n    out[fy1, fx0:fx1 + 1] = frame_c\n    out[fy0:fy1 + 1, fx0] = frame_c\n    out[fy0:fy1 + 1, fx1] = frame_c\n    out[fy0 + 1:fy1, fx0 + 1:fx1] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Draw the frame as solid lines and clear its interior.\")\n    iy0, ix0, iy1, ix1 = fy0 + 1, fx0 + 1, fy1 - 1, fx1 - 1\n    ih, iw = iy1 - iy0 + 1, ix1 - ix0 + 1\n    horizontal = iw >= ih\n    if horizontal:\n        pieces.sort(key=lambda p: p[2])\n        total = sum(p[4] - p[2] + 1 for p in pieces) + len(pieces) - 1\n        pos = ix0 + (iw - total) // 2\n        for c, y0, x0, y1, x1 in pieces:\n            h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n            ty = iy0 + (ih - h) // 2\n            out[ty:ty + h, pos:pos + w] = c\n            pos += w + 1\n            emit(out, f\"Place the solid {h}x{w} block of colour {c} in the row inside the frame.\")\n    else:\n        pieces.sort(key=lambda p: p[1])\n        total = sum(p[3] - p[1] + 1 for p in pieces) + len(pieces) - 1\n        pos = iy0 + (ih - total) // 2\n        for c, y0, x0, y1, x1 in pieces:\n            h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n            tx = ix0 + (iw - w) // 2\n            out[pos:pos + h, tx:tx + w] = c\n            pos += h + 1\n            emit(out, f\"Place the solid {h}x{w} block of colour {c} in the column inside the frame.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The dot colour is the most common non-background colour."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A few lattice dots are recoloured to trace the outline of a rectangle; I'll call this the frame."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 1: the interior width is 9 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 3 + 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 2: the interior height is 11 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 + 1."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the interior width is 15 = 1 + 3 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 3 + 1."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Checking the counts**"}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Each piece becomes a solid block.** The block is the piece's bounding box filled in."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Across the packing axis, each block is centred."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a3dd672096a6023afc26035bcd541245d8ae9c3b49d2a27bc12a5be13eb19011","task_sha256":"5451fa4ddebff3150da24c9976938fd8bfb44bdfa46882a131ce2ae848227f9f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
